 http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\nMetadata is provided by the original archive. This page does not imply that the source document or image is freely reusable.\n\nJing Jin, Huan Xu, Olivia X. M. Yao. 2025-12-02. Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions. https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02324--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2512.02324)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Recently, Keith investigated arithmetic properties for the reciprocals of some false theta functions and posed several conjectures. ... Yao}, year={2025}, eprint={2512.02324}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.NT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02324}, }\nSubmitted 02 Dec 2025\n\n# Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions\n\nJJ\n\nJing Jin\n\nHX\n\nHuan Xu\n\nOX\n\nOlivia X.M. Yao\n\n## Abstract\n\nRecently, Keith investigated arithmetic properties for the reciprocals of some false theta functions and posed several conjectures. In this paper, we prove some parity results for the reciprocals of some false theta functions by using some identities on Ramanujan's general theta function. In particular, our results imply some conjectures of Keith.\n\nView PaperPDF\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{jin2025parityresultsreciprocalsfalse, title={Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions}, author={Jing Jin and Huan Xu and Olivia X. M. Yao}, year={2025}, eprint={2512.02324}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.NT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.02324}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen arrow patterns meet classical patterns | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/408520942_When_arrow_patterns_meet_classical_patterns)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2607.04094 ... Second, we settle the third conjecture (which involves a different arrow pattern) by providing two independent proofs. ... We present this characterization in several ways, including vincular patterns, mesh patterns, and a new object that we call \"arrow patterns.\"\nPreprint\n\n# When arrow patterns meet classical patterns\n\n  * July 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2607.04094\n\nAuthors:\n\nShishuo Fu\n\n  * Chongqing University\n\nZhenghe Yang\n\nZhenghe Yang\n\n## Abstract\n\nSeeking to bridge the structural divide between a permutation's cycle notation and its one-line notation, Berman and Tenner introduced a novel notion of permutation pattern known as the arrow pattern. Recently, Archer and Laudone initiated a systematic study of arrow pattern avoidance, leaving behind three intriguing conjectures. In this paper, we resolve all three conjectures. First, we enumerate all six subclasses of permutations that simultaneously avoid a classical pattern of length 3 and a fixed arrow pattern of length 3, thereby confirming the first two conjectures. Second, we settle the third conjecture (which involves a different arrow pattern) by providing two independent proofs. These proofs rely on a restriction of Biane's bijection to non-nesting involutions and Krattenthaler's bijection from 321-avoiding permutations to Dyck paths, respectively.\n\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (17)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nClassification of bijections between 321- and 132-avoiding permutations\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jan 2008\n  * DISCRETE MATH THEOR\n\n  * Anders Claesson\n  * Sergey Kitaev\n\nIt is well-known, and was first established by Knuth in 1969, that the number of 321-avoiding permutations is equal to that of 132-avoiding permutations. In the literature one can find many subsequent bijective proofs confirming this fact. It turns out that some of the published bijections can easily be obtained from others. In this paper we describe all bijections we were able to find in the literature and we show how they are related to each other (via \"trivial'' bijections). Thus, we give a comprehensive survey and a systematic analysis of these bijections. This allows us to write several familiar statistics as linear combinations of pattern counts, both in terms of a permutation and in terms of its image under the fundamental bijection. We use these enumerations to resolve the question of characterizing so-called \"shallow\" permutations, whose depth (equivalently, disarray/displacement) is minimal with respect to length and reflection length. We present this characterization in several ways, including vincular patterns, mesh patterns, and a new object that we call \"arrow patterns.\" Furthermore, we specialize to characterizing and enumerating shallow involutions and shallow cycles, encountering the Motzkin and large Schröder numbers, respectively.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nThe On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences\n\nChapter\n\n  * Nov 2019\n\n  * Neil J. A. Sloane\n\nView\n\n(q,t)-Catalan numbers: gamma expansions, pattern avoidances, and the (−1)-phenomenon\n\nArticle\n\n  * May 2019\n\n  * Shishuo Fu\n  * Dazhao Tang\n  * Bin Han\n  * Jiang Zeng\n\nThe aim of this paper is two-fold. We first prove several new interpretations of a kind of (q,t)-Catalan numbers along with their corresponding γ-expansions using pattern avoiding permutations. Secondly, we give a complete characterization of certain (−1)-phenomenon for each subset of permutations avoiding a single pattern of length three, and discuss their q-analogues utilizing the newly obtained q-γ-expansions, as well as the continued fraction of a quint-variate generating function due to Shin and the fourth author. Moreover, we enumerate the alternating permutations avoiding simultaneously two patterns, namely (2413,3142) and (1342,2431), of length four, and consider such (−1)-phenomenon for these two subsets as well.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\nSpearman's footrule as a measure of disarray\n\nArticle\n\n  * Jan 1977\n\n  * P. Diaconis\n  * R.L. Graham\n\nView\n\nRestricted Permutations\n\nArticle\n\n  * Dec 1985\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/parity-results-for-the-reciprocals-of-false-theta-functions-2609-ef996d99)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: last month; Recently, Keith investigated arithmetic properties for the reciprocals of some false theta functions and posed several conjectures. ... Paper ID: 2512.02324\n\nBack ArXiv math.NT Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions\n\nArXiv math.NT\n\n# Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions\n\nHuan Xu, Jing Jin, Olivia X. M. Yao\n\n2512.02324 Published Dec 2, 2025\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Dec 02, 2025\n\nCategories: math.NT\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nRecently, Keith investigated arithmetic properties for the reciprocals of some false theta functions and posed several conjectures. In this paper, we prove some parity results for the reciprocals of some false theta functions by using some identities on Ramanujan's general theta function. In particular, our results imply some conjectures of Keith.\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2512.02324\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.NT\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### How to Cite This Paper\n\n[Button: BibTeX] [Button: APA] [Button: MLA] [Button: Chicago] [Button: IEEE]\n\n@article{xu2025parity, title = {Parity results for the reciprocals of false theta functions}, author = {Xu, Huan and Jin, Jing and Yao, Olivia X. M.}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2512.02324}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.NT}, }\n\n### Authors\n\nHX Huan Xu 41 papers JJ Jing Jin 3 papers OX Olivia X. M. Yao 6 papers\n\n## PDF Preview\n\nPDF preview not available Try downloading the PDF instead\n\nPreview PDF Download PDF--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Walsh spectra for a permutation-inverse family of Boolean functions | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2603.28491)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week; # 2603.28491 ... In particular, $f_α$ is bent if and only if $α$ is a noncube in $\\mathbb{F}_q$, proving a conjecture of Li, Li, Helleseth, and Qu. ... To make the external shell input independently checkable, we separate the finite-field dictionary from the cited shell theorem and verify directly the degenerate case $e=2$. ... Subject: Number Theory\n# 2603.28491\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Complete Walsh spectra for a permutation-inverse family of Boolean functions [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthor: Kaimin Cheng\n\nLet $q=2^e$ with $e$ even, and let $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ be the finite field of order $q^2$. Put $d=(q^2+q+1)/3$, and consider the permutation polynomial $$σ(X)=X+X^d+X^{dq}\\in\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}[X].$$ For $α\\in\\mathbb{F}_q^*$, define the Boolean function $$ f_α(x)=\\text{Tr}_{q^2}\\bigl(α(σ^{-1}(x))^3\\bigr),\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb{F}_{q^2},$$ where $\\text{Tr}_{q^2}$ denotes the absolute trace from $\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}$ to $\\mathbb{F}_{2}$. In this paper, we determine all Walsh values of $f_α$ and their multiplicities. In particular, $f_α$ is bent if and only if $α$ is a noncube in $\\mathbb{F}_q$, proving a conjecture of Li, Li, Helleseth, and Qu. The $β\\in\\mathbb{F}_q$ part of the spectrum is handled by an elementary finite-field argument, whereas the $β\\in\\mathbb{F}_{q^2}\\setminus\\mathbb{F}_q$ part is reduced to a Hadamard problem on the trace-zero space. We then identify the resulting word with a normalized odd-dimensional shortened Kerdock shell in the noncube case and with a normalized first shortened Delsarte--Goethals shell in the cube case. To make the external shell input independently checkable, we separate the finite-field dictionary from the cited shell theorem and verify directly the degenerate case $e=2$. As an application, we show that a recent cyclotomic family of Xie, Li, Wang, and Zeng is the same construction in different coordinates.\n\nSubject: Number Theory\n\nPublish: 2026-03-30 14:26:43 UTC--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-07-09&page=74&range=7)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; @misc{2607.04094, author = {Shishuo Fu and Zhenghe Yang}, title = {{W}hen arrow patterns meet classical patterns}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.04094}, note = {arXiv:2607.04094v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... Second, we settle the third conjecture (which involves a different arrow pattern) by providing two independent proofs.\n\n  * When arrow patterns meet classical patterns\n\nShishuo Fu, Zhenghe Yang\n\nJul 07 2026 math.CO cs.DM arXiv:2607.04094v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2607.04094, author = {Shishuo Fu and Zhenghe Yang}, title = {{W}hen arrow patterns meet classical patterns}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2607.04094}, note = {arXiv:2607.04094v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nSeeking to bridge the structural divide between a permutation's cycle notation and its one-line notation, Berman and Tenner introduced a novel notion of permutation pattern known as the arrow pattern. Recently, Archer and Laudone initiated a systematic study of arrow pattern avoidance, leaving behind three intriguing conjectures. In this paper, we resolve all three conjectures. First, we enumerate a